AB  - Large language model (LLM)-based agents are beginning to act directly on-chain, autonomously generating and executing transactions through Smart Accounts while decentralized identifier and verifiable credential (DID/VC) frameworks verify the identities and authorities of these non-human principals—a shift most visible today in decentralized finance (DeFi). This transition exposes an authentication–behavior gap: DID/VC mechanisms verify identity at authentication time but cannot ensure behavioral intent at execution time, enabling cross-layer threats in DID-integrated autonomous on-chain execution. We propose a tri-layer reference architecture comprising the Agent, Trust, and Infrastructure Layers — spanning agent reasoning, credential-based identity, and on-chain execution — and through a structured literature review combined with empirical analysis of a frozen public v1.0 dataset of 735 DeFi incidents with a versioned codebook and row-level provenance, we construct a tri-layer vulnerability taxonomy and derive six composite attack patterns that traverse layer boundaries. Under uniform C/P/Pj criteria, CP-2 and CP-4 are Plausible and the other four are Projected. Failure-mode and coverage-matrix analyses indicate that the two most frequent vulnerability classes, Business Logic Flaw and Price Manipulation, together account for just over half of the 696 single-cause records. However, no surveyed technique family demonstrates variant-complete coverage of either, and existing defenses, being largely layer-isolated, do not span the composite cross-layer paths. From these findings, we formulate four Smart-Account-oriented design principles as prioritized research directions and map each to a candidate ERC-4337/draft ERC-7579 realization pathway with its trade-off.
