AB  - Failure analysis occupies a unique epistemic position at the intersection of materials science, mechanical engineering, and organizational decision-making: simultaneously a retrospective diagnostic practice that decodes the history written on a fracture surface, and a prospective source of design knowledge that prevents recurrence. Yet the canonical workflow has not been systematically re-evaluated in light of three transformative developments: the emergence of deep learning architectures—particularly Vision Transformers (ViT) and Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs)—that transcend the limitations of conventional convolutional approaches; the proliferation of metal additive manufacturing with its characteristic defect populations; and the increasing availability of in-situ nondestructive evaluation data streams that feed into digital twin frameworks for real-time structural health assessment. This critical review addresses that lacuna through a critical synthesis of literature spanning 1970 to 2026. We examine the fracture mechanics framework connecting applied stress, crack size, and material toughness, demonstrating how quantitative fractographic measurements constrain the failure sequence when integrated with the Paris law. The principal fracture modes—ductile microvoid coalescence, transgranular cleavage, intergranular separation, fatigue, creep, and environmentally assisted cracking—are reviewed as signatures of governing micromechanisms rather than as a taxonomic checklist. We introduce the Integrated Failure Analysis Framework (IFAF), a coherent synthesis of seven interconnected analytical pillars: (i) a Multi-Layer Analysis Architecture; (ii) a hybrid Vision Transformer-Physics-Informed Neural Network-Large Language Model architecture; (iii) Fracture Metabolomics; (iv) Temporal Fractography; (v) Digital Twin integration; (vi) Epistemological Gap Analysis with Pre-Mortem techniques; and (vii) Living Standards using Bayesian updating. The framework is demonstrated—not prospectively validated—through ten industrial case studies spanning fatigue, brittle fracture, stress corrosion cracking, hydrogen embrittlement, creep, composite delamination, ceramic fracture, and aerospace engine failure. The Failure Complexity-Consequence Matrix is extended with stakeholder weighting factors and a quantitative Failure Complexity Analysis Index (FCAI). A Fracture Metabolomics Database provides chemo-morphological signatures for sixteen common alloy-mechanism combinations. Ten evidence-based recommendations complete the work, including a call for establishing community-standard benchmarking datasets for AI-based fractography.
