KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Additive manufacturing
AB  - Powder-based additive manufacturing (AM) of metals, principally including powder bed fusion (PBF), directed energy deposition (DED), and binder jetting (BJ), has revolutionised modern manufacturing by offering ultimate design freedom, material efficiency and part customisation. However, metallurgical defects remain a major barrier to the broader adoption of this technology, particularly in safety-critical industries. Conventional non-destructive testing and in-situ monitoring approaches provide partial solutions while often facing high costs, limited scalability, and difficulties in detecting subsurface or process-induced defects particularly in complex geometries. Within the last decade, machine learning (ML) models have emerged as alternatives, offering automated feature extraction, pattern recognition, and real-time defect classification. This comprehensive narrative review synthesises state-of-the-art ML approaches and analyses their applications in detecting, classifying, and mitigating defects in the powder-based AM of metals. The synthesis also yields a cross-process finding: ML-defect evidence is concentrated in powder bed fusion (PBF), while DED and BJ remain sparsely studied. In parallel, recent advances in real-time and in-situ monitoring techniques, including vibration, acoustic, thermal, optical, and multi-sensor frameworks, are explored with a focus on the integration of these techniques with ML to enable closed-loop process control. Several key challenges, particularly relating to data scarcity, sensor limitations, predictive uncertainty and confidence calibration, and the interpretability of complex models, remain to be addressed and are the focus of highlighted future research directions. A four-stage roadmap is also proposed, progressing from offline defect detection through in-situ monitoring and closed-loop correction to industrial qualification and certifiable zero-defect manufacturing.
